WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:03.210
- [Instructor] In other
videos we introduced ourselves

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:05.240
to the idea of orbitals

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.900
and these are various orbitals
in their various subshells

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.560
that you could find in
various shells of an atom.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:14.750
And in this video we're gonna
get a little bit more practice

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:16.080
with electron configuration.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.384
In particular, we're
going to expose ourselves

00:00:18.384 --> 00:00:21.670
to the idea of the Aufbau principle.

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.580
Now Aufbau comes from German.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.800
It means the building principle.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:27.210
It's a very useful way

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:31.700
of thinking about electron
configurations past calcium.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:33.600
Let's just a get little bit warmed up.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.540
What is the electron
configuration of neon?

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.280
Pause this video and think about it

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.980
and as a hint I will give
you the periodic table here.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.800
All right, well neon has
an atomic number of 10

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.540
and if we're talking
about a neutral neon atom

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:50.310
it's gonna have 10 electrons.

00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:54.230
And so, the first two will
that fill that first shell.

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:59.230
So we have 1s2 and then, the
next two are going to fill

00:00:59.769 --> 00:01:03.970
the 2s subshell in your second shell.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:05.470
So then you're gonna have 2s2.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:11.000
And then we have six more
electrons to get to 10

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.187
and that's now going to fill
your 2p subshell, so 2p6.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:19.900
And so what's the order of the subshells

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:21.160
that we just filled?

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:25.503
Well, first we filled
1s, then we filled 2s,

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.220
then we filled 2p

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.870
and you can also see that in
the periodic table of elements.

00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:36.840
In this first row, you're
filling that first shell.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.300
In this second row or this second period,

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:44.110
you are filling that second shell.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:47.693
Now what's going to happen if
we were to go to say, argon?

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.150
So if you're going to go to argon,

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.730
what will that electron
configuration look like?

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:55.047
Pause the video and think about that.

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:58.530
Well, we can use the
noble gas configuration

00:01:58.530 --> 00:01:59.800
or the noble gas notation.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:00.700
We could say, all right we're going to be

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.890
building off of neon.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.650
So we're gonna have the
electron configuration of neon,

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:09.200
but then we're going to add
electrons into our third shell.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.720
So from neon we would
then add two electrons

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.190
into the 3s subshell, 3s2.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.610
And then, to get to 18 electrons,

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:19.550
we're at 12 right now,

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.600
we're gonna have six
more that are going to be

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:26.395
in the 3p subshell, so 3p6.

00:02:26.395 --> 00:02:28.950
So, on this diagram over here

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:31.950
we went from 2s to 2p to fill up neon

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.380
and then as we went to argon

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:35.333
we go to 3s to 3p.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:42.510
Now what would be the electron
configuration of calcium?

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.090
Pause the video and think about it.

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.140
All right, well calcium has 20 protons.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.010
So a neutral calcium
would have 20 electrons.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:53.010
So two more electrons than argon.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.600
So we can build off of argon

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.480
and where are those electrons going to go?

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.940
And this is where the Aufbau
principle is interesting.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:06.890
There is indeed a 3d subshell,

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:08.300
but in the case of calcium

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.280
instead of those two electrons
being in the 3d subshell,

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:16.720
they end up in the 4s subshell.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.120
So calcium's electron
configuration is the same as argon

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:25.450
and instead of it being
3d2 here on top of that

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.750
it goes straight to 4s2.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.580
And so that's why I was
drawing this diagram like this

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:33.680
and you'll often see that

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.720
in an introductory chemistry class.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.360
You fill 1s first, no surprises.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:40.130
You're filling in that first shell.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:41.443
Then you fill 2s.

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:45.720
Then you fill 2p and you
filled your second shell.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.500
Then you go to 3s, once
again no surprises.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.210
Then you go to 3p.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:51.380
Now this is the surprise

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.810
and why this Aufbau diagram is useful.

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.680
For electron configuration purposes,

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.720
if you're thinking about
potassium or calcium,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.113
the extra electrons are now
going to go in the 4s subshell.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.240
So now let's think about what
the electron configuration

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.640
of the scandium would be.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:12.650
Pause this video and think about that.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.850
Well scandium has one
more proton than calcium.

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.580
It has 21 protons and if it is neutral,

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.600
it's also gonna have one more electron

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.240
relative to a neutral calcium atom.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.710
And so, it could have a
similar electron configuration.

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:28.170
So we could base it off of argon.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:32.270
We have two electrons in the 4s subshell,

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.193
so I'll write 4s2

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.536
and the Aufbau principle
would describe that

00:04:37.536 --> 00:04:40.480
and the Aufbau principle,
this little diagram,

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.810
would say, all right that
other electron is going to be

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:47.447
in the 3d subshell, so you do 3d1.

00:04:48.774 --> 00:04:52.450
And this is indeed an accurate
electron configuration

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:53.892
for scandium.

00:04:53.892 --> 00:04:56.790
Now if the Aufbau
principle makes you think

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.890
that you're filling 4s first

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.510
and then you are starting to fill 3d,

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:04.980
if you were actually
building up a scandium atom,

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.410
and that's actually taught
in most chemistry books

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:08.384
and in most classes,

00:05:08.384 --> 00:05:12.080
but actually if people start
with the scandium nucleus

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.530
that has 18 electrons.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.750
So that would have a positive charge,

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:18.350
when they add that first electron

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:20.061
it actually does not go to 4s.

00:05:20.061 --> 00:05:22.570
It goes to 3d.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:25.250
So this electron actually
gets added first.

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:27.750
If you're actually
thinking about building.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.730
But I don't want to confuse you too much.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.370
In this video we're just thinking about

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:33.000
the electron configuration

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:36.062
and for that Aufbau can be very useful.

00:05:36.062 --> 00:05:38.912
Now for electron configuration purposes,

00:05:38.912 --> 00:05:43.912
3d, you then go to 4p, and
then you then go to 5s,

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:48.630
and that's why you might
see this type of a diagram,

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:52.610
once again, in your traditional
first year chemistry books.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:56.390
So the big takeaway here
is the Aufbau principle

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.440
that you'll learn, this type of diagram,

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:00.573
it's useful for electron configuration

00:06:00.573 --> 00:06:03.060
and it might be useful to think about it

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.466
as you're building these
atoms electron by electron,

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:09.336
but if you really want
the precise accurate truth

00:06:09.336 --> 00:06:11.610
once you get beyond calcium

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.490
it gets a little bit more complicated.

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:16.250
Now one other thing that
I want you to appreciate

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:18.740
based on what we just learned is patterns

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:20.940
in the periodic table of elements.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.640
So for which elements are we
building out our S subshell?

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:28.900
Well, you could see that
for all of these elements

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:30.820
right over here,

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.180
these first two columns,

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:34.640
we're building out our S subshell.

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.900
Now it looks like
something is missing there.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.300
Is there something else that
builds out our S subshell?

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.130
Well, from that point of
view we could actually think

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.250
of helium as being right over here

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:46.630
'cause helium, we're
building out that 1s subshell

00:06:46.630 --> 00:06:50.210
and because of that all of
these elements right over here,

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.063
we say that they are in the S block.

00:06:54.170 --> 00:06:58.207
Now, which elements are
building out their P subshells?

00:06:58.207 --> 00:07:02.960
Well, all of these
elements right over here

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.510
are building out their P subshells

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.340
or have it fully built out.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:09.620
And because of that,
all of these elements,

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:13.000
we call these the P block.

00:07:13.000 --> 00:07:15.680
And these elements in the
middle right over here,

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.690
scandium is one of them,

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:20.253
they are called the D block.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:24.270
Now one reason why folks might
have called it the D block

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:26.920
is if you really imagine
the Aufbau principle

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:28.610
as building up atoms,

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.100
it might be tempting to say,

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:33.040
oh well we're building
in the fourth row here,

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:35.290
we're building the 3d subshell

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:38.090
or in the fifth row here we're
building the 4d subshell.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.663
Now we now know that
that actually isn't true,

00:07:41.663 --> 00:07:44.680
but from electron
configuration point of view

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:46.890
it can appear that way

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:50.420
and so that's why it is called the D block

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:51.793
and I will leave you there.

